---
// Safe to edit by hand
// The privacy policy's DERIVED sections (2026-09-26): cookies and how visits
// are measured, Google Search, and how to reach us. Rendered after the Sanity
// body on /privacy, and in the no-singleton fallback, so both say the same.
//
// WHY THESE ARE CODE, NOT SANITY TEXT (CLAUDE.md rule 15). The measurement
// paragraph used to be seeded into the privacyPage document, chosen when
// seed-pages ran, beside a bullet saying "It does not set any cookies of its
// own". The day PUBLIC_GA_ID is set at go-live, the Analytics tag would load
// and set _ga cookies while the stored policy still denied them: a false
// statement to visitors that nothing in the build would flag. So the cookie
// and measurement wording is read from src/lib/analytics-config.ts, the same
// booleans that decide whether each tag renders (src/components/Analytics.astro),
// and the contact line from Site settings. Nobody retypes any of it.
//
// Google's Analytics terms require a policy that discloses the use of
// Analytics and its cookies and says how Google uses data from sites that use
// its services; the GA paragraph links Google's own page for that and its
// opt-out add-on. The paragraph's "not used for advertising" and "not shared"
// are true only while the property keeps Google signals, ads personalisation
// and data sharing off (docs/PENDING.md, the go-live GA item).
import { hasCfAnalytics, hasGa } from '@/lib/analytics-config';

interface Props {
  email: string;
  phone?: string | null;
  /** Each h2's classes. Defaults to PortableTextStatic's h2, so after the
   *  Sanity body the derived sections read as one document with it. */
  h2Class?: string;
  /** Each paragraph's classes. Defaults to PortableTextStatic's normal block;
   *  the fallback passes '' (its own spacing wraps them). */
  pClass?: string;
}

const {
  email,
  phone,
  h2Class = 'mt-14 mb-5 font-body text-h2 font-normal text-foreground scroll-mt-24',
  pClass = 'my-5 font-body text-body leading-[1.72] text-foreground/90',
} = Astro.props as Props;
const link = 'text-link underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-primary';
---

<div>
  <h2 class={h2Class}>Cookies and how visits are measured</h2>
  {
    !hasGa && !hasCfAnalytics && (
      <p class={pClass}>
        This site sets no cookies and runs no analytics. Visits are not counted, and nothing is
        stored on your device for measurement.
      </p>
    )
  }
  {
    hasCfAnalytics && !hasGa && (
      <p class={pClass}>
        This site sets no cookies. Page visits are counted with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which does
        not identify individual visitors.
      </p>
    )
  }
  {
    hasGa && (
      <>
        <p class={pClass}>
          {hasCfAnalytics
            ? 'Page visits are counted with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookies, and also measured with Google Analytics.'
            : 'Page visits are measured with Google Analytics.'}{' '}
          Google Analytics sets cookies on your device (named <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_</code>{' '}
          followed by a stream id) so returning visits are recognised as the same session. It
          records which pages are opened, roughly where in the world the visit came from, and what
          kind of device was used. It is not used for advertising, and the data is not sold or
          shared beyond Google's own processing. These are the only cookies this site sets.
        </p>
        <p class={pClass}>
          You can block these cookies in your browser settings, with any tracker-blocking extension,
          or with Google's{' '}
          <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" class={link}>
            Analytics opt-out add-on
          </a>
          , and the site will work exactly the same. Google explains{' '}
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" class={link}>
            how it uses information from sites that use its services
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }
</div>

<div>
  <h2 class={h2Class}>Google Search</h2>
  <p class={pClass}>
    The church uses Google Search Console to see how this site appears in Google search results:
    which searches show it and how often it is chosen. Search Console works from Google's own search
    data. It sets nothing on your device and adds nothing to this site.
  </p>
</div>

<div>
  <h2 class={h2Class}>How to reach us</h2>
  <p class={pClass}>
    Questions about this policy, or about anything on this site, can go to the church office. Email{
      ' '
    }
    <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class={link}>
      {email}
    </a>
    {phone ? ` or call ${phone}` : ''}.
  </p>
</div>
